var e=[{level:`fresher`,q:{en:`What is the difference between single-mode and multimode fibre?`,hi:`Single-mode aur multimode fibre mein kya farak hai?`},a:{en:`<p><b>Multimode (MMF)</b> has a wide 50 µm core (62.5 µm in old cable). Light travels along many paths, so pulses spread out and reach is limited to hundreds of metres. It uses cheaper 850 nm VCSEL optics such as 1000BASE-SX and 10GBASE-SR. Jackets are orange (OM1/OM2) or aqua (OM3/OM4).</p><p><b>Single-mode (SMF)</b> has a 9 µm core, so only one path exists and signals travel tens of kilometres. It uses 1310/1550 nm lasers such as 1000BASE-LX and 10GBASE-LR. Jackets are yellow. Inside a building or data center MMF is common; between buildings and on campus or WAN links SMF is used.</p>`,hi:`<p><b>Multimode (MMF)</b> ka 50 µm ka chauda core hota hai (purani cable mein 62.5 µm). Light kai raaston par chalti hai, isliye pulses phailte hain aur reach kuch sau metre tak limited rehti hai. Isme saste 850 nm VCSEL optics use hote hain jaise 1000BASE-SX aur 10GBASE-SR. Jackets orange (OM1/OM2) ya aqua (OM3/OM4) hote hain.</p><p><b>Single-mode (SMF)</b> ka 9 µm core hota hai, isliye sirf ek raasta hota hai aur signals dasiyon kilometre jaate hain. Isme 1310/1550 nm lasers use hote hain jaise 1000BASE-LX aur 10GBASE-LR. Jackets yellow hote hain. Building ya data center ke andar MMF common hai; buildings ke beech aur campus ya WAN links par SMF use hota hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Add that both optics and the fibre must match; mixing SR and LR is a classic mistake.`,hi:`Yeh jodo ki dono optics aur fibre match hone chahiye; SR aur LR mix karna classic galti hai.`}},{level:`fresher`,q:{en:`What is the difference between half duplex and full duplex?`,hi:`Half duplex aur full duplex mein kya farak hai?`},a:{en:`<p><b>Half duplex</b>: a device can send or receive, but not both at the same time, like a walkie-talkie. It is used on shared media (hubs, old coax) and needs CSMA/CD to handle collisions.</p><p><b>Full duplex</b>: a device sends and receives at the same time, like a phone call. It needs a point-to-point link such as a switch port, CSMA/CD is disabled and collisions cannot occur. A 100 Mbps full-duplex link can carry 100 Mbps in each direction.</p>`,hi:`<p><b>Half duplex</b>: device bhej sakta hai ya receive kar sakta hai, lekin dono ek saath nahi, walkie-talkie ki tarah. Yeh shared media (hubs, purana coax) par use hota hai aur collisions sambhaalne ke liye CSMA/CD chahiye.</p><p><b>Full duplex</b>: device ek saath bhejta aur receive karta hai, phone call ki tarah. Ise switch port jaisa point-to-point link chahiye, CSMA/CD disabled hota hai aur collisions ho hi nahi sakte. 100 Mbps full-duplex link har direction mein 100 Mbps le ja sakta hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Mention that any collision counter on a full-duplex port signals a problem.`,hi:`Batao ki full-duplex port par koi bhi collision counter problem ka signal hai.`}},{level:`fresher`,q:{en:`When do you use a straight-through cable and when a crossover? What is Auto-MDIX?`,hi:`Straight-through cable kab use karte ho aur crossover kab? Auto-MDIX kya hai?`},a:{en:`<p>PCs, routers and servers are <b>MDI</b> (transmit on pins 1,2); switches are <b>MDI-X</b> (transmit on 3,6). <b>Straight-through</b> connects unlike devices: PC-switch, router-switch. <b>Crossover</b> connects like devices: switch-switch, router-router, PC-PC and PC-router.</p><p><b>Auto-MDIX</b> detects the far end's transmit pairs and swaps internally, so either cable works. It is on by default on Catalyst switches (<code>mdix auto</code>), but on some platforms it needs speed and duplex set to auto.</p>`,hi:`<p>PCs, routers aur servers <b>MDI</b> hain (pins 1,2 par transmit); switches <b>MDI-X</b> hain (3,6 par transmit). <b>Straight-through</b> alag type ke devices jodta hai: PC-switch, router-switch. <b>Crossover</b> same type ke devices jodta hai: switch-switch, router-router, PC-PC aur PC-router.</p><p><b>Auto-MDIX</b> far end ke transmit pairs detect karke andar swap kar leta hai, isliye koi bhi cable chal jaata hai. Catalyst switches par yeh default on hai (<code>mdix auto</code>), lekin kuch platforms par ise speed aur duplex auto par chahiye.</p>`},tip:{en:`Note that 1000BASE-T uses all four pairs, so a gigabit link needs every pair healthy.`,hi:`Note karo ki 1000BASE-T chaaron pairs use karta hai, isliye gigabit link ko har pair theek chahiye.`}},{level:`fresher`,q:{en:`What is PoE and what are the main standards?`,hi:`PoE kya hai aur main standards kaunse hain?`},a:{en:`<p>Power over Ethernet sends DC power over the same UTP cable as data. The switch is the <b>PSE</b>, the phone, AP or camera is the <b>PD</b>. The switch first detects a PoE signature, classifies the device, then applies power, so normal NICs are safe.</p><p>Standards: <b>802.3af</b> 15.4 W, <b>802.3at (PoE+)</b> 30 W, <b>802.3bt</b> 60 W (Type 3) and 90 W (Type 4); Cisco <b>UPOE</b> 60 W and <b>UPOE+</b> 90 W. The switch has a total power budget; <code>show power inline</code> shows usage per port.</p>`,hi:`<p>Power over Ethernet data wali hi UTP cable par DC power bhejta hai. Switch <b>PSE</b> hai, phone, AP ya camera <b>PD</b> hai. Switch pehle PoE signature detect karta hai, device classify karta hai, phir power lagata hai, isliye normal NICs safe hain.</p><p>Standards: <b>802.3af</b> 15.4 W, <b>802.3at (PoE+)</b> 30 W, <b>802.3bt</b> 60 W (Type 3) aur 90 W (Type 4); Cisco <b>UPOE</b> 60 W aur <b>UPOE+</b> 90 W. Switch ka ek total power budget hota hai; <code>show power inline</code> har port ka usage dikhata hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Mention CDP/LLDP power negotiation and the budget; it shows real deployment experience.`,hi:`CDP/LLDP power negotiation aur budget ka zikr karo; isse real deployment experience dikhta hai.`}},{level:`fresher`,q:{en:`What is a collision domain and a broadcast domain? How do hubs, switches and routers affect them?`,hi:`Collision domain aur broadcast domain kya hain? Hubs, switches aur routers unhe kaise affect karte hain?`},a:{en:`<p>A <b>collision domain</b> is the set of devices whose frames can collide. A <b>broadcast domain</b> is the set of devices that receive each other's broadcasts.</p><p>A hub is one collision domain and one broadcast domain. A switch makes every port its own collision domain but, per VLAN, is one broadcast domain. A router separates both: every interface is its own collision and broadcast domain. VLANs also split broadcast domains on switches.</p>`,hi:`<p><b>Collision domain</b> devices ka woh set hai jinke frames collide kar sakte hain. <b>Broadcast domain</b> devices ka woh set hai jo ek dusre ke broadcasts receive karte hain.</p><p>Hub ek collision domain aur ek broadcast domain hai. Switch har port ko apna collision domain banata hai lekin, per VLAN, ek broadcast domain hai. Router dono ko alag karta hai: har interface apna collision aur broadcast domain hai. VLANs bhi switches par broadcast domains todte hain.</p>`},tip:{en:`Be ready to count domains in a small drawing; interviewers often sketch one.`,hi:`Chhote drawing mein domains ginne ke liye ready raho; interviewers aksar ek bana dete hain.`}},{level:`mid`,q:{en:`How does auto-negotiation work, and what happens when one end is hard-coded?`,hi:`Auto-negotiation kaise kaam karta hai, aur jab ek end hard-coded ho to kya hota hai?`},a:{en:`<p>Both ends send fast link pulses advertising their capabilities and pick the highest common mode (1000 full, then 100 full, and so on). On Cisco, hard-coding both speed and duplex disables negotiation on that port.</p><p>The neighbour on auto then uses <b>parallel detection</b> to sense the speed, which usually works, but it cannot sense duplex, so it uses the IEEE default: <b>half duplex at 10/100 Mbps</b>, full at 1000. The result is a duplex mismatch. If both ends are fixed at different speeds, the link stays down. Best practice: auto on both ends, or fixed and identical on both ends.</p>`,hi:`<p>Dono ends apni capabilities advertise karne wale fast link pulses bhejte hain aur sabse ooncha common mode chunte hain (1000 full, phir 100 full, aur aage). Cisco par speed aur duplex dono hard-code karne se us port par negotiation disable ho jaata hai.</p><p>Auto wala neighbour phir <b>parallel detection</b> se speed sense karta hai, jo aam taur par chalta hai, lekin duplex sense nahi kar sakta, isliye IEEE default use karta hai: <b>10/100 Mbps par half duplex</b>, 1000 par full. Result duplex mismatch hai. Agar dono ends alag speeds par fixed hon, link down rehta hai. Best practice: dono ends auto, ya dono ends fixed aur ek jaise.</p>`},tip:{en:`Point out the <code>a-</code> prefix in show interfaces status as the quick way to see what was negotiated.`,hi:`show interfaces status mein <code>a-</code> prefix ko batao, negotiated values dekhne ka quick tareeka.`}},{level:`mid`,q:{en:`Explain runts, giants, CRC errors and late collisions.`,hi:`Runts, giants, CRC errors aur late collisions samjhao.`},a:{en:`<p><b>Runts</b>: received frames under 64 bytes, often fragments from collisions. <b>Giants</b>: frames over the maximum size, usually an MTU mismatch. <b>CRC</b>: the FCS did not match, so the frame was damaged: cabling, noise, dirty fibre, bad optic, or the full side of a duplex mismatch. <b>Late collisions</b>: collisions after the first 64 bytes; on modern networks almost always a duplex mismatch, rarely an over-long cable.</p><p>Read them as patterns on both ends: late collisions on one side plus CRC and runts on the other means duplex mismatch; CRC alone with both sides full means a physical problem.</p>`,hi:`<p><b>Runts</b>: 64 bytes se chhote receive hue frames, aksar collisions ke tukde. <b>Giants</b>: maximum size se bade frames, aam taur par MTU mismatch. <b>CRC</b>: FCS match nahi hua, yaani frame damaged tha: cabling, noise, gandi fibre, kharab optic, ya duplex mismatch ki full side. <b>Late collisions</b>: pehle 64 bytes ke baad collisions; modern networks par lagbhag hamesha duplex mismatch, kabhi kabhi bahut lamba cable.</p><p>Inhe dono ends par patterns ki tarah padho: ek side late collisions plus dusri side CRC aur runts matlab duplex mismatch; dono sides full hon aur sirf CRC ho to physical problem.</p>`},tip:{en:`Always add "after clear counters" so the interviewer knows you check whether errors are current.`,hi:`Hamesha "clear counters ke baad" jodo taaki interviewer jaane ki aap check karte ho errors abhi ke hain ya nahi.`}},{level:`mid`,q:{en:`What do administratively down, down/down, up/down and err-disabled mean, and how do you recover an err-disabled port?`,hi:`Administratively down, down/down, up/down aur err-disabled ka kya matlab hai, aur err-disabled port kaise recover karte ho?`},a:{en:`<p>Administratively down: the <code>shutdown</code> command. Down/down: no Layer 1 signal (cable, far end, optic, speed mismatch). Up/down: Layer 1 fine but Layer 2 failing, mostly serial encapsulation or clocking. Err-disabled: the switch disabled the port because a feature such as port security, BPDU Guard, UDLD or link-flap detection triggered.</p><p>Recovery: check <code>show interfaces status err-disabled</code> and the log for the reason, fix the cause, then <code>shutdown</code> / <code>no shutdown</code>, or configure <code>errdisable recovery cause ...</code> with an interval (300 s default).</p>`,hi:`<p>Administratively down: <code>shutdown</code> command. Down/down: Layer 1 signal nahi (cable, far end, optic, speed mismatch). Up/down: Layer 1 theek lekin Layer 2 fail, zyada tar serial encapsulation ya clocking. Err-disabled: switch ne port disable kiya kyunki port security, BPDU Guard, UDLD ya link-flap detection jaisa feature trigger hua.</p><p>Recovery: reason ke liye <code>show interfaces status err-disabled</code> aur log check karo, cause fix karo, phir <code>shutdown</code> / <code>no shutdown</code>, ya interval ke saath <code>errdisable recovery cause ...</code> configure karo (default 300 s).</p>`},tip:{en:`Stress fixing the root cause first; otherwise the port err-disables again immediately.`,hi:`Pehle root cause fix karne par zor do; warna port turant dobara err-disable ho jaata hai.`}},{level:`mid`,q:{en:`How does a switch build its MAC address table and decide where to send a frame?`,hi:`Switch apni MAC address table kaise banata hai aur tay karta hai ki frame kahan bhejna hai?`},a:{en:`<p>It <b>learns</b> the source MAC of every frame against the ingress port and VLAN, and refreshes the entry's aging timer (300 s default). Then it looks up the destination MAC: if known on another port, it <b>forwards</b> out that port only; if known on the same port, it <b>filters</b>; if unknown, broadcast or unknown multicast, it <b>floods</b> to all ports in the VLAN except the ingress.</p><p>Verify with <code>show mac address-table</code>. Entries age out, and a MAC that appears on a new port is simply moved.</p>`,hi:`<p>Woh har frame ka source MAC ingress port aur VLAN ke against <b>seekhta</b> hai, aur entry ka aging timer refresh karta hai (default 300 s). Phir destination MAC dhoondta hai: agar dusre port par known hai, sirf usi port se <b>forward</b>; agar same port par known hai, <b>filter</b>; agar unknown, broadcast ya unknown multicast hai, VLAN ke saare ports par ingress ko chhod kar <b>flood</b>.</p><p><code>show mac address-table</code> se verify karo. Entries age out hoti hain, aur naye port par dikhne wala MAC bas move ho jaata hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Say "learn from source, forward on destination" in the first sentence; it is what they listen for.`,hi:`Pehle hi sentence mein "source se seekho, destination par forward karo" bolo; interviewers yahi sunna chahte hain.`}},{level:`senior`,q:{en:`Why is the minimum Ethernet frame 64 bytes, and why do late collisions point to a duplex mismatch?`,hi:`Minimum Ethernet frame 64 bytes kyun hai, aur late collisions duplex mismatch ki taraf ishara kyun karte hain?`},a:{en:`<p>In CSMA/CD a sender only detects a collision while it is still transmitting. The slot time, 512 bit times, is the worst-case round trip on a maximum-size collision domain, so every frame must last at least 512 bits, which is 64 bytes. Any legitimate collision therefore happens within the first 64 bytes.</p><p>In a duplex mismatch the full-duplex side transmits whenever it wants. The half-duplex side may already be deep into a long frame when it hears that, so it records a collision after 64 bytes: a late collision. The full side receives the aborted frame as a runt or CRC error. On a correctly cabled network late collisions have no other common cause except over-long segments.</p>`,hi:`<p>CSMA/CD mein sender collision tabhi detect karta hai jab woh abhi bhi transmit kar raha ho. Slot time, 512 bit times, maximum-size collision domain par worst-case round trip hai, isliye har frame kam se kam 512 bits chalna chahiye, yaani 64 bytes. Isliye har legitimate collision pehle 64 bytes ke andar hota hai.</p><p>Duplex mismatch mein full-duplex side jab chahe transmit karti hai. Half-duplex side tab tak ek lambe frame mein kaafi aage ho sakti hai jab woh ise sunti hai, isliye woh 64 bytes ke baad collision record karti hai: late collision. Full side ko aborted frame runt ya CRC error ki tarah milta hai. Sahi cabled network par late collisions ka bahut lambe segments ke alawa koi aur common cause nahi hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Linking the frame size to CSMA/CD timing shows depth beyond memorised facts.`,hi:`Frame size ko CSMA/CD timing se jodna rate kiye facts se aage ki depth dikhata hai.`}},{level:`senior`,q:{en:`Compare store-and-forward and cut-through switching. How does it affect troubleshooting CRC errors?`,hi:`Store-and-forward aur cut-through switching compare karo. Isse CRC errors troubleshoot karne par kya asar padta hai?`},a:{en:`<p><b>Store-and-forward</b> receives the whole frame and checks the FCS before forwarding, so corrupted frames are dropped at the first switch and counted there. <b>Cut-through</b> starts forwarding once it has read the destination MAC, which lowers latency but can pass a corrupted frame onward; the error may then be counted on a downstream switch or host, far from the bad cable.</p><p>So in cut-through fabrics you trace CRC errors back hop by hop (many platforms also count "stomped" CRC separately) to find where they originate. Fragment-free waits for the first 64 bytes as a compromise.</p>`,hi:`<p><b>Store-and-forward</b> poora frame receive karta hai aur forward karne se pehle FCS check karta hai, isliye corrupted frames pehle switch par hi drop aur gine jaate hain. <b>Cut-through</b> destination MAC padhte hi forward karna shuru karta hai, jisse latency kam hoti hai lekin corrupted frame aage ja sakta hai; error phir kisi downstream switch ya host par gina ja sakta hai, kharab cable se door.</p><p>Isliye cut-through fabrics mein aap CRC errors ko hop by hop peeche trace karte ho (kai platforms "stomped" CRC alag se bhi ginte hain) taaki pata chale woh kahan se shuru hue. Fragment-free compromise ke taur par pehle 64 bytes ka intezaar karta hai.</p>`},tip:{en:`Mentioning where errors are counted proves you have debugged real fabrics.`,hi:`Errors kahan gine jaate hain yeh batana prove karta hai ki aapne real fabrics debug kiye hain.`}},{level:`senior`,q:{en:`You are cabling a new campus building with Wi-Fi 6E access points and a 10G uplink to a core 1.2 km away. What do you specify?`,hi:`Aap ek naye campus building ki cabling kar rahe ho jismein Wi-Fi 6E access points aur 1.2 km door core tak 10G uplink hai. Aap kya specify karoge?`},a:{en:`<p>Uplink: 1.2 km rules out copper and multimode, so single-mode OS2 fibre with 10GBASE-LR SFP+ optics (10 km reach) and LC connectors, ideally two diverse paths for redundancy. Clean and inspect connectors, and baseline DOM light levels.</p><p>Access: Cat6A to the APs so they can run multigigabit (2.5/5GBASE-T, or 10G later) within 100 m, and access switches with <b>PoE+ or UPOE</b> ports and enough total PoE budget, possibly dual power supplies. Keep all runs under 100 m, place IDFs accordingly, and document every uplink with descriptions.</p>`,hi:`<p>Uplink: 1.2 km copper aur multimode ko rule out karta hai, isliye 10GBASE-LR SFP+ optics (10 km reach) aur LC connectors ke saath single-mode OS2 fibre, redundancy ke liye ideally do alag paths. Connectors saaf aur inspect karo, aur DOM light levels ka baseline lo.</p><p>Access: APs tak Cat6A taaki woh 100 m ke andar multigigabit (2.5/5GBASE-T, ya baad mein 10G) chala sakein, aur <b>PoE+ ya UPOE</b> ports aur kaafi total PoE budget wale access switches, shayad dual power supplies ke saath. Saare runs 100 m ke andar rakho, IDFs usi hisaab se lagao, aur har uplink ko descriptions se document karo.</p>`},tip:{en:`Justify each choice by distance, speed and power; that is what a design answer needs.`,hi:`Har choice ko doori, speed aur power se justify karo; design answer ko yahi chahiye.`}},{level:`scenario`,q:{en:`Users behind a router complain of slowness. The router's LAN interface is up/up, shows Half-duplex, 100Mb/s and hundreds of late collisions. What is happening and how do you fix it?`,hi:`Router ke peeche users slowness ki complain karte hain. Router ka LAN interface up/up hai, Half-duplex, 100Mb/s aur sainkdon late collisions dikhata hai. Kya ho raha hai aur aap kaise fix karoge?`},a:{en:`<p>Duplex mismatch. The router is probably on auto and fell back to half because the switch port is hard-coded (for example 100/full) and stopped negotiating. Confirm on the switch: <code>show interfaces status</code> will show <code>full 100</code> without <code>a-</code>, and <code>show interfaces</code> will show CRC and runts; CDP may log DUPLEX_MISMATCH.</p><p>Fix: set the switch port to <code>speed auto</code> and <code>duplex auto</code> (or hard-code the router to match). Then <code>clear counters</code>, run an extended ping, and confirm late collisions and CRC stay at zero.</p>`,hi:`<p>Duplex mismatch. Router shayad auto par hai aur half par gir gaya kyunki switch port hard-coded hai (jaise 100/full) aur usne negotiate karna band kar diya. Switch par confirm karo: <code>show interfaces status</code> bina <code>a-</code> ke <code>full 100</code> dikhayega, aur <code>show interfaces</code> CRC aur runts dikhayega; CDP DUPLEX_MISMATCH log kar sakta hai.</p><p>Fix: switch port ko <code>speed auto</code> aur <code>duplex auto</code> par set karo (ya router ko match karne ke liye hard-code karo). Phir <code>clear counters</code>, extended ping chalao, aur confirm karo ki late collisions aur CRC zero par rahein.</p>`},tip:{en:`Point out that the side with late collisions is not the side with the wrong configuration.`,hi:`Batao ki late collisions wali side galat configuration wali side nahi hai.`}},{level:`scenario`,q:{en:`A new fibre uplink stays notconnect. Both SFPs are recognised. How do you troubleshoot?`,hi:`Ek naya fibre uplink notconnect par ruka hai. Dono SFPs recognise ho rahe hain. Aap kaise troubleshoot karoge?`},a:{en:`<p>Check that both optics are the same type and wavelength (SR with SR on multimode, LR with LR on single-mode) and match the installed fibre. Read <code>show interfaces transceiver</code>: normal Tx but very low Rx (around -40 dBm) means no light arrives, typically reversed polarity (Tx connected to Tx), a wrong patch or a broken strand. Swap the LC pair at one end, clean and inspect connectors, check patch panel mapping and APC/UPC mismatch.</p><p>Also confirm both ports are not shut down and speed settings are compatible. Verify with the port coming up and Rx power in the optic's normal range.</p>`,hi:`<p>Check karo ki dono optics same type aur wavelength ke hain (multimode par SR ke saath SR, single-mode par LR ke saath LR) aur installed fibre se match karte hain. <code>show interfaces transceiver</code> padho: normal Tx lekin bahut kam Rx (lagbhag -40 dBm) ka matlab light pahunch hi nahi rahi, aam taur par ulti polarity (Tx Tx se juda), galat patch ya toota strand. Ek end par LC pair swap karo, connectors saaf aur inspect karo, patch panel mapping aur APC/UPC mismatch check karo.</p><p>Yeh bhi confirm karo ki dono ports shutdown nahi hain aur speed settings compatible hain. Port up hone aur Rx power ke optic ki normal range mein hone se verify karo.</p>`},tip:{en:`Quoting DOM values as evidence sounds far stronger than "I would reseat it".`,hi:`DOM values ko saboot ki tarah quote karna "main reseat karunga" se kahin zyada strong lagta hai.`}},{level:`scenario`,q:{en:`A server port shows a-full a-1000 on both ends, but CRC errors keep rising on the switch side only. What do you do?`,hi:`Ek server port dono ends par a-full a-1000 dikhata hai, lekin CRC errors sirf switch side par badh rahe hain. Aap kya karoge?`},a:{en:`<p>Both ends are full duplex and there are no collisions, so this is not a duplex issue; it is physical damage on frames travelling from the server to the switch. Clear counters to confirm the errors are current. Replace the patch cord, reseat at both ends, run a TDR cable test (<code>test cable-diagnostics tdr interface ...</code>) to check pairs, check for EMI sources and cable length, and try another switch port or server NIC to isolate the transmitter.</p><p>After each change, clear counters and watch. Declare it fixed only when CRC stays at zero under normal load.</p>`,hi:`<p>Dono ends full duplex hain aur collisions nahi hain, isliye yeh duplex issue nahi; yeh server se switch ki taraf jaane wale frames par physical damage hai. Errors abhi ke hain yeh confirm karne ke liye counters clear karo. Patch cord badlo, dono ends par reseat karo, pairs check karne ke liye TDR cable test (<code>test cable-diagnostics tdr interface ...</code>) chalao, EMI sources aur cable length check karo, aur transmitter isolate karne ke liye dusra switch port ya server NIC try karo.</p><p>Har change ke baad counters clear karke dekho. Tabhi fixed declare karo jab normal load mein CRC zero par rahe.</p>`},tip:{en:`Explaining that errors counted on the receiver point to the opposite transmit path is a strong senior signal.`,hi:`Yeh samjhana ki receiver par gine errors ulte transmit path ki taraf ishara karte hain ek strong senior signal hai.`}}];export{e as default};